WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.500
Part 2. You will hear a guide at a tourist attraction called Onerton Hall talking to a group of visitors.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.500
First you have some time to look at questions 11 to 14.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:47.500
Now listen carefully and answer questions 11 to 14.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:57.500
Good morning and welcome to Onerton Hall, one of the largest estates in the area.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.000
My name's Nick and I'm one of the guides.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:08.000
I'll give you a brief introduction to the estate while you're sitting down and then we'll walk round.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:18.000
The estate consists of the house, gardens, parkland and farm and it dates back to the 14th century.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:27.000
The original house was replaced in the late 17th century and of course it has had a large number of owners.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Almost all of them have left their mark, generally by adding new rooms, like the ballroom and conservatory, or by demolishing others.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:49.000
The farm looks much as it's always done, although the current owner has done a great deal of work to the flowerbeds.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:57.000
In the 17th century the estate was owned by a very wealthy man called Sir Edward Downes.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:05.000
His intention was to escape from the world of politics after years as an active politician

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:13.000
and to build a new house worthy of his big collection of books, paintings and sculptures.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:25.000
He broke off contact with his former political allies and hosted meetings of creative and literary people, like painters and poets.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:35.000
Unusually for his time, he didn't care whether his guests were rich or poor, as long as they had talent.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Big houses like Onerton had dozens of servants until the 1920s or 30s and we've tried to show what their working lives were like.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Photographs, of course, don't give much of an idea, so instead, as you go round the house,

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:04.000
you'll see volunteers dressed up as 19th century servants going about their work.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:12.000
They'll explain what they're doing and tell you their recipes or what tools they're using.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:20.000
We've just introduced this feature to replace the audio guide we used to have available.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.000
I see there are a number of children here with you today.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:34.000
Well, we have several activities specially for children, like dressing up in the sorts of clothes that children wore in the past.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:42.000
And, as it's a fine day, some of you will probably want to play in the adventure playground.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:50.000
Our latest addition is child-sized tractors that you can drive around the grounds.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Before you hear the rest of the talk, you have some time to look at questions 15 to 20.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:35.000
Now listen and answer questions 15 to 20.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:43.000
We'll also be going into the farm that's part of the estate, where there's plenty to do.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:53.000
Most of the buildings date from the 18th century, so you can really step back into an agricultural past.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:02.000
Until recently, the dairy was where milk from the cows was turned into cheese.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:11.000
It's now the place to go for lunch, or afternoon tea, or just a cup of coffee and a slice of homemade cake.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:21.000
The big stone building that dominates the farm is the large barn, and in here is our collection of agricultural tools.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:29.000
These were used in the past to plough the earth, sow seeds, make gates, and much more.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:38.000
There's a small barn, also made of stone, where you can groom the donkeys and horses to keep their coats clean.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:45.000
They really seem to enjoy having it done, and children love grooming them.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:56.000
The horses no longer live in the stables, which instead is the place to go to buy gifts, books, our own jams and pickles,

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:00.000
and clothes and blankets made of wool from our sheep.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:12.000
Outside the shed, which is the only brick building, you can climb into a horse-drawn carriage for a lovely relaxing tour of the park and farm.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.000
The carriages are well over a hundred years old.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:27.000
And finally, the parkland, which was laid out in the 18th century with a lake and trees that are now well established.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:34.000
You'll see types of cattle and sheep that are hardly ever found on farms these days.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:39.000
We're helping to preserve them, to stop their numbers falling further.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:44.000
Okay, well, if you'd like to come with me, we will start...

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:55.000
That is the end of part two. You now have half a minute to check your answers to part two.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.000
Thank you.

